 ## Background
-Vicinity shading was proposed by Stewart in 2003 as a volumetric version of ambient occlusion [S2, unofficial]. It simulates the illumination of isosurfaces by taking into account neighboring voxels. The method was developed to provide natural-looking lighting effects in a faster way than global illumination, which is particularly important for interactive volume visualization [S1]. Ambient occlusion is a simplified version of the obscurances illumination model [S1]. Vicinity shading has been used in medical imaging and other volume visualization applications to enhance visual quality and generate saliency maps for view selection [S1][S2, unofficial].
